lear Democratic edge in this D+2 open seat. The single high-quality poll shows a four-point race and DiLorenzo is campaigning as a Trump moderate, so the expected margin lands closer to seven points.", "watch": "If DiLorenzo's moderate pitch consolidates independents or the national environment cools, this becomes a genuine tossup; a surge of Democratic outside spending could instead push it toward Safe D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's 1st District is an open seat with Chris Pappas running for Senate, and both parties nominated well-funded newcomers: Stefany Shaheen, daughter of the state's Democratic senator, and Republican Anthony DiLorenzo. The district leans Democratic by only about two points and has a long history of swing elections, so the seat is genuinely contestable even in a good Democratic year.\n\nRatings cluster at Likely D, with a few raters at Lean D or Safe D, and prediction markets put Shaheen's chances around 90 percent. The single recent poll, from the University of New Hampshire in late September, shows Shaheen up four among likely voters, a narrower margin than most other indicators imply. Shaheen holds a cash-on-hand advantage and benefits from roughly $2.2 million in outside support, while DiLorenzo has emphasized distance from Trump on tariffs and tone. A Democratic win of about seven points is the expectation, with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8928, "margin": 7.5}]}